  "question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project aims to integrate a comprehensive authentication solution for Rails applications, leveraging the Devise gem. Devise is a flexible and modular authentication framework based on Warden, providing a complete Model-View-Controller (MVC) solution via Rails engines. It is Rack-based and supports multiple user models signed in simultaneously. By modular design, developers can customize the authentication features to their specific needs.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nTo implement a robust authentication system with essential security and usability features for Rails applications. The system should support multiple authentication modules, ensuring a secure and user-friendly experience.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Implement and maintain the authentication system within Rails applications.\n- End Users: Register, sign in, and manage their accounts securely.\n- Administrators: Oversee user account security and assist in account recovery and management.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Database Authenticatable: Securely hash and store user passwords for authentication. Supports login via POST requests or HTTP Basic Authentication.\n- Omniauthable: Integrate third-party authentication systems via OmniAuth.\n- Confirmable: Send account confirmation emails and verify user accounts upon registration.\n- Recoverable: Enable password resets and send reset instructions via email.\n- Registerable: Facilitate user registration, account editing, and deletion processes.\n- Rememberable: Manage user session persistence through cookies with token generation and clearing.\n- Trackable: Monitor user sign-in statistics, including count, timestamps, and IP address.\n- Timeoutable: Automatically expire sessions inactive for a predetermined period.\n- Validatable: Validate user email and password inputs, allowing customization.\n- Lockable: Secure accounts by locking after failed sign-in attempts, with unlock options via email or time period.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- The system must efficiently handle concurrent user sessions with a focus on security and performance.\n- Modular configuration should allow developers to enable only required modules, optimizing system resource usage.\n- Email communications must be secure and reliable for user confirmations and notifications.\n- Account lockout and unlock processes should be timely and user-friendly.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0043\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL DEFAULT '',\n  `encrypted_password` varchar(255) NOT NULL DEFAULT '',\n  `reset_password_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `reset_password_sent_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `remember_created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `sign_in_count` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `current_sign_in_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_sign_in_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `current_sign_in_ip` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_sign_in_ip` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `confirmation_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `confirmed_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `confirmation_sent_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `unconfirmed_email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `failed_attempts` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `unlock_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `locked_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `provider` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `uid` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_users_on_email` (`email`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_users_on_reset_password_token` (`reset_password_token`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_users_on_confirmation_token` (`confirmation_token`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_users_on_unlock_token` (`unlock_token`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_users_on_provider_and_uid` (`provider`,`uid`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
